<td id="S5.T4.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56.07_{\pm 1.92}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>56.07</mn><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1.92</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56.07_{\pm 1.92}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">HCPD (Ours, Pre-RL)</td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66.54_{\pm 0.59}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>66.54</mn><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.59</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66.54_{\pm 0.59}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">HCPD (Ours, Post-RL)</td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86.25_{\pm 1.08}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>86.25</mn><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1.08</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86.25_{\pm 1.08}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transferability across target models.</span>
A key advantage of HCPD is its <em id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">model-agnostic</em> design: <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">it operates in the natural language space, which is universally shared across LLMs, thereby achieving superior transferability across diverse target models</span>. To demonstrate the advantages of HCPD, we further compare it with other training-based baselines under a transfer setting where the detector is evaluated on outputs generated by target models from different families and scales. Results in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Zero-source LLM Hallucination Detection with Human-like Criteria Probing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show that when applied to unseen target models, competing baselines such as HaloScope and TSV often suffer noticeable performance degradation due to distributional shifts in features of the proxy model. In contrast, HCPD maintains stable and excellent performance across heterogeneous target models, which undoubtedly better meet the needs of practical deployment in open, real-world settings. More results on the other <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> datasets are shown in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 More Results on Transferability across Target Models ‣ Appendix D More Experiment Results ‣ Zero-source LLM Hallucination Detection with Human-like Criteria Probing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.12900v1/ablation_reward_design.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/214;" width="333" height="214"></object>
